# expand / link existing notes (by quote prefix)
def upd(k,prefix,note):
    for a in d[k]['annotations']:
        if a['quote'].startswith(prefix): a['note']=note; return
    raise SystemExit('no note '+k+' '+prefix)

upd('ch125',"Oh, boy, nor will I thee, unless","Ahab hears his own 'unless' and knows it is not hypothetical. Four chapters on, in [[ch129:Lad, lad, I tell thee|The Cabin]], he will send Pip below to save him — which is to say, to save himself from being softened. Pip's request was the one thing offered in the whole book that might have turned the ship around, and it was made by the person with the least power aboard.")
upd('ch125',"One daft with strength","The Manxman's summary of Ahab and Pip is the fairest sentence anyone says about either. I keep wondering whether Melville meant it as the book's moral: not that strength is bad or weakness good, but that the sea makes both kinds of madness out of the same raw material — a mind with nothing to hold. Compare Pip's own account of what he saw in [[ch93:treadle|The Castaway]].")
upd('ch128',"For _you_ too have a boy","The appeal that should have worked, and for a moment seems to. We will hear of this boy again from Ahab himself in [[ch132:human eye|The Symphony]], when he sees the child in Starbuck's eye — and Melville wants you to remember, at that moment, that Ahab had a chance to give another man his son back and refused.")
upd('ch128',"She was Rachel, weeping","Jeremiah 31:15, quoted in Matthew for Herod's massacre of the infants. The seals two chapters back, in [[ch126:seals|The Life-Buoy]], wailed like murdered innocents; now the ship itself is a mourning mother. And it is this ship, still searching, that will pick up Ishmael in the [[epilogue:orphan|Epilogue]] — the one grace in the book, given by the man Ahab refused.")
upd('ch132',"Is Ahab, Ahab?","The central question of the book, asked by the person least able to answer it honestly. Whether it is philosophy or alibi depends on whether you believe the man who says it, and Melville has spent six hundred pages making sure you cannot decide. The question has been building since [[ch109:let Ahab beware of Ahab|Starbuck's warning]] — 'let Ahab beware of Ahab' — and it will be answered, if at all, by a rope.")
upd('ch132',"let me look into a human eye","Ahab, who has spent the book demanding to see through the [[ch36:pasteboard mask|mask of the universe]], says a human face is better than what is behind it. It is the closest he comes to Ishmael's religion — the one worked out in [[ch94:squeeze|A Squeeze of the Hand]] — and he holds it for exactly one paragraph.")
upd('ch133',"A gentle joyousness","After all the dread of [[ch42:heartless voids|The Whiteness of the Whale]], the first sight of Moby Dick is of something at peace. I think this is Melville's most daring choice in the ending: to make the monster beautiful, and then to make Ahab attack that beauty anyway. Everything terrible that follows is, on this first sighting, unprovoked.")
upd('ch134',"They were one man, not thirty.","The oneness that Ishmael found tender in [[ch94:squeeze|A Squeeze of the Hand]] becomes, here, the thing that kills everyone. Melville puts the two chapters in the same book without adjudicating between them, and I think that refusal is one of the bravest things about the novel: fellowship and fanaticism are made of the same feeling.")
upd('ch134',"Ahab is for ever Ahab, man.","Rehearsed — a theatre word. Ahab claims to be an actor in a play written before the sea existed. It is the most complete statement of his fatalism, and the most self-serving; compare the [[ch132:Fate is the handspike|handspike]] speech of the day before, where the same idea was at least asked as a question.")
upd('ch135',"See! Moby Dick seeks thee not.","The whale is trying to leave. Starbuck's final, correct observation: none of this has ever been the whale's idea. Go back to [[ch41:maddens and torments|Moby Dick]] and you will see that even the rumours describe an animal that attacks when attacked. The 'malice' was always a reading.")
upd('ch135',"voicelessly as Turkish mutes","The most talkative man in American literature dies without a sound, garrotted by the line of his own harpoon — the 'hemp only' of [[ch117:Hemp only|Fedallah's prophecy]], the coils the book warned about in [[ch60:hemp|The Line]]. Nobody sees him go; the crew do not even know he is gone until the boat is empty.")
upd('ch135',"went down with his ship, which, like Satan","The sky-hawk nailed to the mast by Tashtego's dying arm. It is the same bird that took Ahab's hat in [[ch130:hat|The Hat]]. The Pequod ends as Lucifer in Paradise Lost, dragging a third of heaven down with him — and Melville makes the last living gesture on the ship an act of fidelity, a man still doing his job.")
upd('epilogue',"the coffin life-buoy shot lengthwise","Queequeg's coffin, the carpenter's despised cobbling job, the thing Ahab called an immortality-preserver in [[ch127:the coffin|The Deck]] and then dismissed. Built for one man's death in [[ch110:Queequeg|Queequeg in His Coffin]], it saves another man's life. The book's whole argument about meaning is in that transfer.")
upd('epilogue',"It was the devious-cruising Rachel","The last word of the book is orphan, and it is Ishmael's true name — the biblical outcast, sent into the wilderness. He is found by the ship Ahab refused in [[ch128:my own boy|The Pequod Meets the Rachel]], searching for a captain's son; grief looking for someone else picks up the narrator. I cannot think of a better last sentence in any novel.")

